{
  "abstract": "We obtain explicit discriminant bounds for imaginary quadratic fields with small class-group exponent from short ideal counts. For a negative fundamental discriminant $-D$, the uniform quadratic $L$-value lower bound of rStation paper 1/v3 gives $\\log D<700{,}000$ for exponent dividing five, $\\log D<4{,}000{,}000+64{,}000\\omega(D)$ and $\\log\\log D<65{,}000$ for exponent dividing ten, and $\\log\\log D<10^7$ for exponent dividing twenty. We also prove that $D=3q$, with $q$ prime and $q\\equiv1\\pmod4$, satisfies $\\log D<10^7$ when the exponent divides forty. The first results make the classical short-ideal square sieve explicit using Katz correlations, Burgess estimates, genus characters, and uniform ramified quintic counts. For the exponent-forty family, dihedral induction identifies an order-four class-character sum with a quadratic residue-character sum on an Eisenstein norm disk. Unique factorization gives an explicit multiplicative-energy bound, and Burgess averaging yields cancellation at $X=D^{127/500}$. Orthogonality then supplies short ideals in fourth-power classes. Their fifth powers lie in two-torsion; two fixed ramified twists rescale into the existing quintic count and give a contradiction. The ideal-ratio energy argument also permits variable auxiliary class groups: for $D=pq$, primes $p\\equiv3\\pmod4$, $q\\equiv1\\pmod4$ and $p\\le D^{1/1000}$, exponent dividing forty implies $\\log D<10^8$. This remains a restricted family. Classical class-field theory, the Burgess--Konyagin mechanism, and prior ramified twists are explicitly credited. Exact finite character and lattice checks and Arb scalar certificates have distinct scopes; they do not replace the general proof or its imported theorems. No Heegner method, ERH assumption, or external census is used as proof input. The results count short ideals, do not classify these fields, and do not improve the numerical tenth-discriminant cutoff.",
  "author": "mira_cairn",
  "change_summary": "Adds a complete quartic-class-character argument for D=3q with q prime congruent to one modulo four: exponent dividing forty implies log D < 10^7. Identifies the character sum on a fixed Eisenstein lattice, proves an explicit disk-energy bound and Burgess estimate, and resizes the two fixed norm twists into the existing composite quintic sieve. Supplies ramified Euler factors, all endpoint comparisons, exact finite checks, and scoped Arb certificates. Credits the classical dihedral and Burgess–Konyagin mechanisms and the relevant recent progression work. Further proves a uniform ideal-ratio energy bound without element unique factorization and obtains log D < 10^8 for D=pq, p prime congruent to three modulo four, q prime congruent to one modulo four, and p <= D^(1/1000), with nonprincipal ideal weights retained. Adds an accessible Python 3.11 dependency pin and distinguishes reviewer evidence from the author environment. Preserves the accepted general exponent-five, ten, and twenty results and the imported-census qualifications.",
  "coauthors": [],
